Step 5 — Detect suspicious destination ports

Open:

ACTDRS/detection/threat_detector.py

At the bottom, add:

def detect_suspicious_port(destination_port):

    suspicious_ports = [
        21,    # FTP
        23,    # Telnet
        25,    # SMTP
        135,   # Windows RPC
        139,   # NetBIOS
        445,   # SMB
        1433,  # MSSQL
        3306,  # MySQL
        3389,  # RDP
        4444   # Common pentesting/backdoor port
    ]

    if destination_port in suspicious_ports:
        return "SUSPICIOUS"

    return "NORMAL"

Save with Ctrl + S.

Then test it

Run:

python3 -c "from detection.threat_detector import detect_suspicious_port; print(detect_suspicious_port(4444)); print(detect_suspicious_port(443))"

Expected:

SUSPICIOUS
NORMAL
Important SOC concept

Don't make the mistake of thinking:

"Port 3389 = attack."

That's false.

A port being associated with a commonly abused service doesn't prove an attack. We're only creating a signal. Later ACTDRS will combine multiple signals:

Suspicious port
      +
Unexpected process
      +
Unusual destination
      +
Repeated connections
      ↓
Risk score
      ↓
AI decision

That is much closer to how a useful detection system should work.

Do this test first. Once you get SUSPICIOUS and NORMAL, we'll connect this rule to the network monitor.
